From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lena <lena@x>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:05:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] lena-0151: flush the console ring from panic(), safe process dump

Two panics (0148 at bsd_init, 0149 in userspace 35 s in) both left the
persistent ring ending one line BEFORE the interesting one: a panic stops
every other thread, and the CPU then spins in the debugger, so the flush
thread never writes the tail - including the panic text itself. Reading a
panic therefore still means photographing the panel.

sweet_early_panic() now copies the panic message into the console ring and
calls sweet_log_panic_flush(), which takes the UFS lock with try_lock (the
panicking thread may be the one holding it) and writes the ring plus the
header synchronously. While that runs, ufs_scsi() is told not to sleep
(sweet_log_in_panic): a blocked command would never be woken now, and a
4 KiB write completes in microseconds, so the poll loop is enough.

The flush position moves from the thread's stack into file scope so both
paths share it.

The process dump drops the uu_wmesg string: two boots panicked inside the
first dump even after it was moved past bsd_init, and a %s of a stale or
bogus wait-message pointer is the obvious fault. It prints pid, state,
command and the raw wait-channel address only, plus a closing count and
the free-page count (which used to be in the header, printed before the
panic).
